/* Track: Commitment and friction. Ordered foundational first.
 *
 * Defaults work on your inattention. This track works on the opposite case:
 * the moment you are paying full attention and want out. It is unusually well
 * served by randomised trials, and nearly all of them were run in low-income
 * cash economies, so the external validity question is stated inside each
 * evidence field rather than waved through. */
(window.LESSONS = window.LESSONS || []).push(
{
  id: "commitment-planner-and-doer",
  track: "commitment", level: "judgement",
  title: "A plan your later self will want to break needs a mechanism rather than more resolve",
  source: "Richard Thaler and Hersh Shefrin, An Economic Theory of Self-Control (Journal of Political Economy, 1981)",
  gateIntro: "It is the second week of January. The transfer you set up on New Year's Day has run once. Last night you moved eight hundred dollars back out of the savings account to cover a flight you had known about since October, and it took you under a minute on the train. The transfer runs again on Thursday. You are sitting with a coffee, aware that nothing in the arrangement objected and nothing in it will object in March either.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that would have made last night slower, not the resolution that would have made it wrong.",
  evidence: "No trial. Thaler and Shefrin propose a model rather than run an experiment: one person is treated as an organisation containing a farsighted planner and a myopic doer, and self-control is whatever the planner does to constrain the doer, either by changing the incentives it faces or by removing its options altogether. The framing is the contribution. Its weakness is that a two-selves model can be fitted to almost any behaviour after the event, which makes it very close to unfalsifiable as stated. Everything in this track that has real evidence behind it is a test of a device this paper described, not a test of the paper.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Defaults work on your inattention, and commitment devices work on the opposite problem: the moments when you are paying full attention and want to defect.",
  why: "The defaults literature has a very specific shape. It works because you do not act, and it is measured on populations that reliably do not act. That is why its effect sizes are the largest in personal finance and also why it stops exactly where it stops. A default governs what happens in the absence of a decision. It has nothing at all to say about the Tuesday night when you sit down, look at the balance, decide the holiday matters more, and put the money back where it came from.\n\nThe planner and doer framing is what covers that gap. Treat yourself as two parties with different horizons. The one that sets up an arrangement in summer and the one that reverses it in winter are both you, both fully informed, and both acting on their preferences at the time. On that reading willpower is not the mechanism and neither is information. The mechanism is that the party with the longer horizon gets to shrink the option set of the party with the shorter one, in advance, while it still holds the pen.\n\nThat turns a psychological problem into an engineering one, which is the only reason it is useful. You are not trying to want it more in six months. You are trying to arrange today that the version of you in six months has fewer buttons available, or that the buttons cost something to press. That is a different design brief from the one a default answers, and almost every entry that follows is an attempt at it.\n\nThe honest cost of the framing is that it explains too much. Any lapse can be relabelled as the doer winning, and a story that fits every outcome has told you nothing about which device to build. So it earns its place only as a generator of testable arrangements, and the value of this track sits in the ones that got tested.",
  failureMode: "You save diligently for eleven months and take the lot back out in the twelfth to cover something you had known about since the start, and the balance ends the year roughly where it began. Nothing malfunctioned. The transfer ran twenty-four times, every one of them succeeded, and at no point in those eleven months did the money become any harder to reach than it was on day one.",
  worked: "The instruction is not about the amount. It is about who can reverse it, and how fast:\n\n\"Open the savings account at an institution I hold nothing else with, and set the transfer to run from my everyday account into it the day after each pay deposit.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is every statement of intent. No target, no rule about what the money is for, no promise not to withdraw. All of those are instructions addressed to a party that will not be in the room in six months. The only clause that survives contact with that party is the one that makes a reversal take a day and a fresh login rather than a thumb on a train.",
  experiment: "Open your savings account history and find every withdrawal you made from it in the last twelve months. Beside each one write how long the withdrawal took you to execute, in minutes. If every answer is under two minutes, you do not have a commitment arrangement, you have a second everyday account, and the cheapest available change today is to move the balance somewhere that adds a day to the exit.",
  reflection: "What did you take back out of savings in the last year, and how long did executing it take?",
  recall: {
    q: "Defaults have the largest effects in personal finance, so what is left for a commitment device to do?",
    a: "Defaults operate on the absence of a decision, and they are measured on populations that reliably make none. They arrange what happens while you are not looking. They do nothing about the moment you are looking, know precisely what you are doing, and reverse the arrangement because the nearer want has won.\n\nThe planner and doer framing treats that moment as a negotiation between two parties inside one person with different horizons, which turns the job into shrinking your later self's option set while the earlier one still holds the pen. It is a model rather than a finding, and it explains any lapse after the fact, so its worth is the devices it suggests rather than anything it establishes."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is every account I hold and how fast I can move money out of each one. Tell me which balances my later self can raid in under two minutes, and what it would take to slow that down without leaving me short in a bad month."
},
{
  id: "commitment-restricted-account",
  track: "commitment", level: "standing",
  title: "An account you cannot withdraw from raised savings substantially, and most people offered one said no",
  source: "Nava Ashraf, Dean Karlan and Wesley Yin, Tying Odysseus to the Mast: Evidence from a Commitment Savings Product in the Philippines (Quarterly Journal of Economics, 2006)",
  gateIntro: "The emergency fund has sat between two and three thousand dollars for four years. It has been to four thousand twice. Both times something entirely reasonable happened, a dentist and a car battery, and both times you moved the money in the queue while the card machine waited. The account pays a decent rate. It is also the second row on the transfer screen you look at every day.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that puts a day between you and that balance, including where it goes and what releases it.",
  evidence: "A randomised offer, not a randomised product. Existing and former clients of a rural bank in the Philippines were randomly assigned to be offered a SEED account, which blocked withdrawals until either a date or a savings amount that the client chose had been reached, and which paid no more interest than the ordinary account. Balances after twelve months were substantially higher among those offered it than among controls, and a third group that got the same visit and the same encouragement to save without the product is what separates the restriction from the attention. Two limits bound the reading. Take-up was a minority of those offered, so the headline is an intention-to-treat estimate spread across many people who declined, and the effect on people who actually held one is larger but not cleanly identified. And the setting is a rural Philippine bank serving low-income clients with few alternative places to put money, which is a long way from a salaried Australian with an offset account and a phone.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "A savings account whose only distinguishing feature is that you cannot get at the money raises balances, which means the restriction is the active ingredient rather than the return.",
  why: "Start with what the SEED account was. Same interest as the ordinary account, less access. On every dimension a standard model prices, it was strictly worse than the product the client already had. A person whose preferences today match their preferences next year should never choose it, and the ones who did were revealing something specific: that they expected to disagree with themselves, and would rather remove the option now than argue with it later. Demand for a dominated product is the diagnosis, and the balance increase is the evidence that the product treated the condition.\n\nThe third arm is what makes it more than a story. Being visited by a bank employee and told to save more is itself a treatment, and plenty of interventions in this field turn out to be attention wearing a product's clothes. Separating the encouragement from the restriction is what licenses the claim that the lock did the work.\n\nWhat transfers to you is not the magnitude, which belongs to that bank and those clients. It is the design. A restriction chosen by the saver rather than imposed on them, a release condition that is either a date or an amount, and no return penalty for accepting it. In Australia there is no product on the shelf shaped like that, so the equivalent has to be assembled: a different institution, no card attached, and no saved payee pointing back the other way.",
  failureMode: "Four years of an emergency fund that has never held above about three thousand dollars, because every time it does, something reasonable happens and the transfer takes eleven seconds at the counter. The interest rate on that account was never the constraint. Its position on the transfer screen was.",
  worked: "The Australian version has to be built rather than bought, and the release condition is the part people leave out:\n\n\"Open a savings account at an institution where I hold nothing else, transfer $500 the day after each pay, do not add it as a payee anywhere, and do not order a card for it. The money is released on 1 December or at $12,000, whichever comes first.\"\n\nThe release condition is doing real work. An open-ended restriction is one you eventually break with no rule covering it, and the first unruled breach is what teaches you the arrangement is decorative. A named date or a named amount gives it a legitimate ending, which is exactly the choice the clients in the trial were offered.",
  experiment: "Count the taps between your everyday banking screen and a completed transfer out of savings. If it is fewer than five, spend twenty minutes today opening an account at a second institution and move across the part of the balance you are confident you will not need this month. Write the release condition into the account nickname, as a date or an amount, so it is visible every time you log in.",
  reflection: "How many taps was it, and what release condition did you write into the nickname?",
  forecast: { q: "Will the balance I move to a separate institution today still be sitting there, untouched, in ninety days?" },
  recall: {
    q: "The SEED account paid the same interest as an ordinary account and gave up access. Why is the fact that anyone chose it informative?",
    a: "Because on any model where your preferences today match your preferences next year it is a dominated product and nobody should take it. Choosing it is a statement that you expect to disagree with yourself and would rather delete the option while you still can. The take-up is itself the diagnosis.\n\nThe twelve-month balance increase is the other half, and the arm that received the same visit without the product is what rules out attention as the explanation. But take-up was a minority and the estimate is averaged over everyone offered, so the effect on actual holders is larger and less cleanly identified, and the setting is a rural Philippine bank rather than anywhere with an offset account."
  },
  deepDive: "I want to build the Australian equivalent of an account I cannot withdraw from, without an interest penalty and without locking up money I might genuinely need. Here is what I hold and what my worst month looks like. Design the restriction and tell me where it breaks."
},
{
  id: "commitment-present-bias-over-money",
  track: "commitment", level: "judgement",
  title: "The impatience used to justify locking money away shows up clearly for effort and much less clearly for money",
  source: "David Laibson, Golden Eggs and Hyperbolic Discounting (Quarterly Journal of Economics, 1997), against Augenblick, Niederle and Sprenger, Working over Time: Dynamic Inconsistency in Real Effort Tasks (Quarterly Journal of Economics, 2015)",
  gateIntro: "You have a spreadsheet open comparing a five-year term deposit against your current savings rate, and the difference over the whole term comes to about four hundred dollars. It is Sunday afternoon. In another tab, unfinished since February, is the super rollover form from when you changed jobs. The spreadsheet is considerably more interesting than the form, and you have three hours.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that closes the gap between deciding something and it running, with a named day in it.",
  evidence: "Laibson's contribution is a model, not a trial: discounting that falls steeply between now and soon and gently between later and later still, which produces preferences that reverse as the near date arrives and makes an asset you cannot easily reach genuinely valuable. The empirical question is whether people show that pattern over money, and the cleanest test splits the answer. Augenblick, Niederle and Sprenger had the same participants allocate both unpleasant tasks and money across dates within one design, and found substantial present bias in the allocation of effort and very little in the allocation of money. One reading is that money is fungible with the rest of a person's budget, so an experimental monetary allocation can be undone outside the experiment and the design cannot see the bias. Another is that the bias is about doing things rather than about consumption timing. Either way the empirical claim under savings commitment is weaker than the model it is used to support, and this is one careful study rather than a settled literature.",
  replication: "contested",
  idea: "Your intertemporal problem is less about preferring money now and more about preferring to do nothing now, so put the commitment on the actions rather than on a general resolve to be patient.",
  why: "The standard story runs like this. People discount the near future much harder than the far future, so a plan that looks good from a distance stops looking good when it arrives, and an asset you cannot reach protects the plan from the arrival. That story is why illiquidity is worth paying for, and it is the engine under every commitment savings product.\n\nThe awkward result is that when someone measured both in the same people at the same time, the bias was there for effort and largely absent for money. That does not prove patience. Money in an experiment is fungible with money outside it, so a participant can offset any allocation the design imposes, which means a null over money is what you would see whether or not the bias exists. But it does mean the specific empirical foundation is softer than the confidence with which it gets asserted.\n\nThe useful part is that the design implication survives either interpretation, and it points somewhere slightly different from where the literature usually points. The reliable failure is not that you chose cash over compounding. It is that the arrangement never got set up, and that a specific salient purchase beat an abstract future balance. So commitment aimed at effort, which mostly means automation and a named day, has firmer ground under it than commitment aimed at your patience.\n\nWhich also tells you what not to spend Sunday on. An hour comparing rates is an hour spent on the dimension where you are apparently already fine.",
  failureMode: "Eight months of an unsigned rollover form. Two super accounts the whole time, two sets of administration fees and one duplicate insurance premium, quietly billed monthly. The gap you were optimising in the spreadsheet was four hundred dollars over five years. The gap the unfinished form opened was larger and it arrived every month.",
  worked: "Write the instruction against the effort rather than against the impatience:\n\n\"Anything I decide on gets set up in the same sitting as the decision, or it is not decided.\"\n\nThat is a rule rather than a mandate, so it needs one enforcement clause or it will not hold:\n\n\"If it cannot be set up in that sitting, it goes into the calendar on a named day inside a fortnight, with the amount and the account already written into the entry title.\"\n\nWhat is absent is any target return, any hurdle rate and any promise about patience. The evidence that you are impatient about abstract money is contested. The evidence that arrangements do not get set up is in your own account history.",
  experiment: "List every financial decision you made in the last six months and, beside each, the date the corresponding arrangement actually started running. Some will have no second date. That gap is your real discount problem, expressed in months. Take the one with the largest gap and set it up now, before you finish the list.",
  reflection: "What was the largest gap between a decision you made and the day it started running?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why does the finding that present bias is weak over money not remove the case for commitment devices?",
    a: "Because money in an experiment is fungible with money outside it, so participants can offset whatever allocation the design imposes, and a small measured bias over money is consistent with both a real bias the design cannot see and no bias at all. The result softens the foundation without demolishing it.\n\nAnd because the same study found substantial present bias over effort, which relocates the commitment problem rather than dissolving it. The dependable failure is the arrangement that never got set up and the specific purchase that beat an abstract balance, so devices aimed at effort, meaning automation and a named day, rest on firmer evidence than devices aimed at your patience."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are the financial decisions I have made in the last year and which ones are actually running. Diagnose whether my problem is impatience or unstarted admin, then give me one arrangement that attacks whichever it turns out to be."
},
{
  id: "commitment-take-up-is-a-minority",
  track: "commitment", level: "judgement",
  title: "Commitment devices work for the minority who sign one, and most people offered a device decline it",
  source: "Take-up rates reported across the commitment device trials, including Ashraf, Karlan and Yin (Quarterly Journal of Economics, 2006) and Gine, Karlan and Zinman (American Economic Journal: Applied Economics, 2010)",
  gateIntro: "Your brother-in-law announces at a barbecue that he has put half his bonus into a five-year term deposit and cannot get at it, and he is visibly pleased with himself. You know with total certainty that you would not have done that. You also know that you have turned down a notice-period saver, a fixed rate and a salary sacrifice in the last three years, each time for a reason that sounded fine on the day.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the mildest restriction you would genuinely accept today, and name the clause you had to leave out to make it acceptable.",
  evidence: "The regularity is about who says yes. In the Philippine commitment savings trial, a minority of the clients offered the restricted account opened one. In the smoking cessation deposit contract run in the same country, a small fraction of the smokers offered it signed. The pattern recurs across commitment products in savings, in smoking, in exercise: the device does something for the people who sign, and most people do not sign. That is why reported effects are usually intention-to-treat estimates averaged over a largely untreated group, and why the effect among takers is both larger and harder to attribute, since takers choose themselves. What no trial in this class can tell you is whether you are one of the people it helps, because they randomise the offer and never the person.",
  replication: "replicated",
  cheat: "Design the restriction you will actually accept rather than the strictest one on offer: across the commitment trials, only a minority of those offered a device ever signed one.",
  idea: "The question that decides your outcome is not whether commitment devices work but whether you are someone who will sign one, and the base rate says probably not.",
  why: "Two robust facts sit awkwardly together. The devices move the outcome they target. Most people refuse them. A product with a real effect and a minority uptake is a decent business and a poor policy, and for one person it means the binding constraint is not the availability of restrictions but their own willingness to be restricted.\n\nWhy people decline is the interesting half, because it is not ignorance. The offer gets explained, often by a bank employee sitting in front of them. Refusing a restriction is perfectly reasonable if you value the access, or if you do not think you have a self-control problem. The third case is the one worth naming: you think you might have one and would rather not be held to anything about it. That is why the sentence \"I will just be disciplined about it\" survives so much contrary evidence, including your own.\n\nSo the take-up rate is best read as a prior about yourself, and you already have your own. Every restrictive arrangement you have ever been offered and declined is a data point, and if the list is long the honest conclusion is not that you need a stricter device. It is that the reachable design is the mildest restriction you will actually accept, installed today, with a stricter clause held back until the mild one has survived a bad month.\n\nThe same arithmetic cuts the other way on the published numbers. Because the estimates average over everyone offered, they understate what happens to holders. If you are the sort of person who signs, the headline is closer to a floor than to a forecast.",
  failureMode: "You read this literature, agree with all of it, and install nothing, because every concrete arrangement available to you had one clause you would not wear. A year later you are a line in a take-up statistic and your balances are exactly where they would have been if you had never heard of any of it.",
  worked: "This instruction is written to be accepted, not to be optimal:\n\n\"Move the savings balance to an institution with no card attached, so a withdrawal takes a day, and start the transfer this pay cycle.\"\n\nNotice what has been left out, because it is the part that would have made you say no. No penalty. No notice period. No minimum balance. No fixed term. Every one of those is available and every one of them raises the chance you decline today, and a restriction you accept beats a stricter one you refuse. If the mild version holds for six months without incident, that is the moment a harder clause becomes worth offering yourself.",
  experiment: "Write down every restrictive financial arrangement you have been offered in the last five years: a term deposit, a notice-period saver, a salary sacrifice, a higher insurance excess, a fixed rate. Mark which ones you accepted. That proportion is your personal take-up rate. Then install one arrangement today that is mild enough to land on the accepted side of your own line.",
  reflection: "What is your own take-up rate on restrictions you have been offered, and which clause usually makes you decline?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why is a device with minority take-up still worth taking seriously for one person, and what does that take-up rate cost the published estimate?",
    a: "Because the estimate is measured across everyone offered, most of whom declined, so it is diluted by a large untreated group. The effect on people who actually held the device is larger, which means that if you are the kind of person who signs, the headline number is closer to a floor than to a forecast.\n\nWhat it costs is causal cleanliness in the other direction. Takers select themselves, so the bigger effect among them mixes the device with the type of person who chooses it, and no trial randomises the person. The practical use of the take-up rate is as a prior about yourself: if you have declined every restriction ever put in front of you, the design problem is finding the mildest one you will accept, not the strictest one that exists."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are the restrictive financial arrangements I have accepted and declined over the last five years. Tell me what my own pattern says about which commitment device would actually survive contact with me, then pick one and write it."
},
{
  id: "commitment-harsher-penalty-attracts-more",
  track: "commitment", level: "standing",
  title: "Offered a menu of savings accounts, people put more money into the one with the harsher exit penalty",
  source: "John Beshears, James Choi, Christopher Harris, David Laibson, Brigitte Madrian and Jung Sakong, Which Early Withdrawal Penalty Attracts the Most Deposits to a Commitment Savings Account?",
  gateIntro: "Two savings products sit on the same page of your banking app. One pays the headline rate and releases money the moment you ask. The other pays the same rate but wants thirty-one days notice before anything moves. You are about to point the automatic transfer at one of them, and you have been on this screen for a while, because the notice period feels like a trap and you cannot name what for.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that names which account the transfer lands in, and what you will do on the day you want the money early.",
  evidence: "An experiment in which participants allocated a real sum between a fully liquid account and commitment accounts that charged a penalty for withdrawing early, with the size of that penalty varied across conditions and the accounts otherwise the same. More of the money went into the accounts carrying the larger penalty. That is the wrong direction for a person with consistent preferences and no self-control problem: a penalty on an otherwise identical account is pure cost, so the liquid option should have taken everything. What the design cannot support is a magnitude for your life. It is an allocation of a bounded sum over a bounded horizon by participants recruited for a study, and what somebody allocates once from a researcher's menu is not what they maintain against a bad month three years later.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "When the same money is offered in a liquid and a restricted form at the same return, more of it goes to the restricted one, because people are buying the height of the wall rather than pricing a cost.",
  why: "Take the standard prediction seriously first, because the result only means something against it. Two accounts, identical in every respect except that one charges you to leave early. The charge is a cost with no compensation attached, so a person whose preferences are stable across time should allocate nothing to it, and should allocate less as the charge rises. The observed allocation went the other way, and rose with the severity.\n\nThe reading is that participants were not comparing prices, they were comparing promises. A penalty is not experienced as a worse deal, it is experienced as a stronger guarantee that the money will still be there, and somebody who expects to be tempted will pay in liquidity for a taller wall. That is the same logic as the restricted account in the Philippines, arrived at from the opposite direction: there, choosing a dominated product revealed the problem, and here, choosing a more heavily penalised one reveals how much wall people want.\n\nThe caution worth carrying is the tension with what happens in the field. Offered a commitment device against the option of doing nothing, most people decline. Offered a menu of commitment devices with no do-nothing option, they lean towards the tighter one. Both can be true, and the reconciliation is probably the menu itself, which quietly deletes the status quo from the choice set.\n\nThat reconciliation is the transferable part. Do not ask yourself whether to restrict the money. Ask which restriction, out of two or three you have already written down, and make the weakest one on the list still a restriction.",
  failureMode: "The transfer has pointed at the instant-access account for three years because that was the destination already selected in the dropdown, and every one of the nine withdrawals you have made from it took under a minute. The notice-period version was on the same page the entire time, at a comparable rate, and nothing about your contribution would have had to change.",
  worked: "The instruction names the destination, because the amount is already running:\n\n\"Point the automatic transfer at the notice-period saver, and keep only the month's buffer in the instant-access account.\"\n\nThen the clause that decides whether it survives its first test:\n\n\"If I want the money early, I lodge the notice and wait the thirty-one days. I do not move the transfer back.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any product that charges a fee to leave. A notice period costs you time and nothing else, so the worst case is an inconvenience rather than a loss, and an arrangement whose worst case is an inconvenience is one you will still be running next year.",
  experiment: "Open your own bank's savings product list and find out whether a notice-period account exists at a comparable rate. If it does, open it today, change the destination of your existing transfer, and leave one month of expenses behind in the instant-access account. The whole change takes about ten minutes and does not touch the amount you contribute.",
  reflection: "Which account was your transfer pointing at, and had you ever actually chosen it?",
  recall: {
    q: "A withdrawal penalty on an otherwise identical account should attract no money at all. What happened instead, and what does it tell you about how to present yourself with a choice?",
    a: "More money went into the accounts with the harsher penalty, and the share rose with the severity of the penalty. Participants were not pricing a cost, they were buying a promise, and a bigger penalty reads as a stronger one.\n\nThe design lesson is to give yourself a menu of restrictions rather than a choice between a restriction and the status quo. In the field, offered a device against the option of doing nothing, most people decline; handed a set of devices with no do-nothing option, they lean tighter. It is one allocation experiment over a bounded sum, so take the direction and leave the magnitude."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are the savings and offset products available to me and their rates. Tell me which combination gives me the most friction for the least cost, and what my worst month does to it."
},
{
  id: "commitment-earmark-and-partition",
  track: "commitment", level: "standing",
  title: "Money in a separate labelled container survives, and it survives better when the label names what it is for",
  source: "Pascaline Dupas and Jonathan Robinson, Why Don't the Poor Save More? Evidence from Health Savings Experiments (American Economic Review, 2013), and Dilip Soman and Amar Cheema, Earmarking and Partitioning: Increasing Saving by Low-Income Households (Journal of Marketing Research, 2011)",
  gateIntro: "The savings account says eleven thousand two hundred dollars, and you are looking at a flight to Japan that costs three. Registration falls due in March. The insurance excess exists only in theory until it does not. Somewhere inside that figure is the four you told yourself last year was untouchable, and you cannot tell which dollars are which, because there is exactly one number on the screen.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that splits that number up, naming each container and what lands in it each pay.",
  evidence: "Two randomised trials, two countries, two populations, one direction. Dupas and Robinson randomly allocated savings technologies of differing restrictiveness to members of rotating savings groups in rural Kenya, among them a lockable box earmarked for health, and found increases in health savings and in spending on preventive health products. Soman and Cheema paid low-income labourers in India part of their wages in sealed envelopes and found that partitioning the same money into more envelopes rather than fewer increased how much of it was still there later, with a version carrying an image of the household's children doing better again. What replicates is the direction and the mechanism, not a number. Both are low-income cash economies with weak formal banking, the horizons are months rather than decades, and no equivalent trial has been run on a salaried professional whose money is a row in an app rather than a note in an envelope. That external validity question is genuinely open and should not be waved past.",
  replication: "replicated",
  cheat: "Give money its own named account before you need it: earmarking and partitioning raised savings in randomised trials, all of them run in low-income cash economies.",
  idea: "A partition turns spending from a slope you slide down into a step you have to take, and the name on it is part of the barrier rather than administrative tidiness.",
  why: "Spending down a single balance is continuous. Every dollar is the same decision as the one before it, no particular dollar is the one that broke the plan, and there is no moment at which anything visibly happens. That is the shape of most people's savings account, and it is a shape with no natural stopping point in it.\n\nA partition changes the geometry. Opening the second envelope is discrete, dated and knowable: you did a thing, and you know you did it. That is why more envelopes beat fewer envelopes holding the identical amount of cash, which is a result no model of money as a fungible quantity can produce. The partition is not storing the money differently. It is inserting events into a process that previously had none.\n\nThe label works on a separate channel. A container with a purpose written on it cannot be raided without reclassifying it, and reclassification is a small act of self-contradiction that you have to perform consciously. The photograph of the children is the extreme version of that, and the fact that it worked is uncomfortable rather than surprising.\n\nThe honest question is what survives the translation to an app. Less than you would like: tapping a sub-account is not tearing an envelope. But two properties do carry, and they are free. The money is no longer part of the number you consult when deciding what you can afford, and it has a name specific enough that moving it requires you to say what you are now calling it.",
  failureMode: "One account holding the holiday, the insurance excess, the registration and the emergency fund, presenting itself as a single figure of eleven thousand dollars. In March the registration and the excess both land in the same fortnight, and it turns out you were never saving for either of them. You were spending from a total that never told you what it was carrying.",
  worked: "This one is four instructions, and the naming is the load-bearing part:\n\n\"Split the savings account into named accounts: Registration and insurance, Emergency, Tax, Holiday. Point a separate automatic transfer at each one the day after pay, sized to the annual bill divided by the number of pays before it falls due.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is a total. No screen you use should show the sum of those four, because a total is the thing people spend from. The names are dull on purpose too: an account called Emergency is measurably harder to raid for a long weekend than one called Savings, because Savings is a category so broad that every withdrawal is consistent with it.",
  experiment: "Write down, from memory and before you look, what your savings balance is currently carrying: which annual bills, which planned purchases, which reserve. Then open the account and compare. Most banks allow several named savings accounts at no cost, so do the split today and set one transfer per account, each sized to the bill divided by the pays remaining before it lands.",
  reflection: "What was your single balance actually carrying, and how far out was your guess?",
  forecast: { q: "In ninety days, will each named account still hold only the money it was named for?" },
  recall: {
    q: "Why did splitting the same amount of cash into more envelopes leave more of it intact, when the total was identical?",
    a: "Because a single balance is spent down continuously, with no dollar distinguishable from the next and no moment at which anything happens. Partitioning inserts discrete events: opening the second envelope is a thing you did and know you did, which is a stopping point that a slope does not have.\n\nThe label is a second, separate barrier. Money with a purpose written on it cannot be taken without reclassifying it, and that is a small conscious act of self-contradiction. Both results come from randomised trials in low-income cash economies over horizons of months, so the direction is well supported and the transfer to a salaried professional with a banking app is an assumption, not a finding."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is my single savings balance and every irregular bill and goal it is silently carrying. Split it into named accounts with a per-pay amount for each, and tell me which of them I will raid first."
},
{
  id: "commitment-forfeitable-deposit",
  track: "commitment", level: "standing",
  title: "A contract that destroys your money if you fail changes behaviour, and a large share of the people who sign one forfeit",
  source: "Xavier Gine, Dean Karlan and Jonathan Zinman, Put Your Money Where Your Butt Is: A Commitment Contract for Smoking Cessation (American Economic Journal: Applied Economics, 2010)",
  gateIntro: "The super rollover form has been open in the same browser tab since February. So has the insurance comparison, and the will you started after your daughter was born. You have told your partner three separate times that this weekend is the weekend. It is Saturday morning, they have taken the kids to swimming, you have four uninterrupted hours, and nothing distinguishes today from the last three Saturdays.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction as a contract: what gets done, by what date, and who keeps your money if it does not.",
  evidence: "A randomised offer at a bank in the Philippines. Smokers were offered a voluntary account into which they deposited their own money over six months, refundable only if they passed a urine test for nicotine at the end, and forfeited to charity if they failed. Those offered the contract were more likely to pass than controls, by a few percentage points off a low base, and the gap was still visible at a surprise test some months after the contract had finished, which is the result that matters most. Take-up was a small minority of the smokers offered it. A large share of those who did sign failed the test and lost their deposits, so a contract that worked in aggregate moved money away from many of the individuals who chose it. One bank, one country, self-selected takers, and an effect measured on being offered rather than on holding the contract.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "Putting your own money behind a dated obligation works, and part of why it works is that the money is genuinely gone if you fail, which gives the device a real expected cost.",
  why: "A promise to yourself has no counterparty, no date and no receipt, which is why it can be renegotiated silently and usually is. A forfeitable deposit fixes all three at once. There is a date, there is a third party holding the money, and failing produces an event that somebody else observes. The behaviour has not become more attractive. Failing has become expensive and visible.\n\nThe surprise test is the finding worth carrying. Plenty of interventions hold behaviour in place for exactly as long as they are running and then release it, which makes them scaffolds rather than changes. Here the difference persisted after the contract had ended and the money had been settled either way, which is weak but genuine evidence that something outlasted the incentive.\n\nAnd then the part that gets left out when this study is quoted approvingly. Many of the people who signed lost their deposits. The expected cost of the device to a taker is the failure rate multiplied by the stake, and that product is not small. So the stake has to be sized against two constraints at once: large enough that failing hurts, and small enough that losing it does not damage the thing you were staking on. Never stake money that was already committed to the goal, because then failure costs you twice.\n\nApplied to a financial life rather than to smoking, the target is not a habit. It is the dated administrative act you have been carrying for months, where the entire barrier is that nothing happens if you do not do it.",
  failureMode: "Four consecutive years of promising yourself this is the year you sort the insurance, the will and the second super account. Nothing is dated, nothing is staked, nobody else knows. Each December all three are still open, and the fourth year alone cost you twelve more months of an overpriced premium and twelve more months of fees on a fund you were not choosing to be in.",
  worked: "A contract has three parts and most people write only the first:\n\n\"I am giving Sam $300 today. If the rollover form is not lodged by the thirtieth, Sam keeps it.\"\n\nTwo details carry the weight. Sam has to genuinely keep it, or send it somewhere you actively dislike, because a stake that quietly comes back is a promise with extra paperwork. And the amount has to be money you can lose without derailing the goal, because the trial's least quoted finding is how many people lost theirs. Staking a month of savings is not more serious, it is just a worse bet.\n\nWhat is absent is any clause about trying hard. The contract is indifferent to effort and settles only on the observable outcome, which is the whole reason it beats a resolution.",
  experiment: "Pick the single financial task you have carried unfinished for longest. Name a date inside three weeks, name a person, and hand them today an amount you would genuinely rather not lose. Tell them in writing what happens when the date passes. If you cannot find a person willing to keep the money, this is not a commitment contract, so pick a smaller task and finish it this hour instead.",
  reflection: "What did you stake, who is holding it, and what date did you name?",
  forecast: { q: "Will the task I staked money on be finished by the date I named?" },
  recall: {
    q: "The smoking contract's most important result was not the pass rate at the end of the contract. What was it, and what does the forfeiture rate add to the picture?",
    a: "That the difference between the offered group and the controls was still visible at a surprise test after the contract had ended and the deposits had been settled. Many interventions hold behaviour only while they run; this one showed weak evidence of something that outlasted the incentive.\n\nThe forfeiture rate is the price of admission. A large share of the people who signed failed and lost their money, so the expected cost to a taker is the failure rate times the stake. That sets the sizing rule: enough that failing hurts, little enough that losing it does not damage the goal, and never money that was already committed to the goal itself."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is the financial admin I have been carrying unfinished, with how long each item has been open. Turn the worst one into a dated forfeitable contract, size the stake against my income, and tell me how I would wriggle out of it."
},
{
  id: "commitment-reminders-are-small",
  track: "commitment", level: "standing",
  title: "A reminder to save raises saving by a few percent, which is real, small, and nothing like what it is sold as",
  source: "Dean Karlan, Margaret McConnell, Sendhil Mullainathan and Jonathan Zinman, Getting to the Top of Mind: How Reminders Increase Saving (Management Science, 2016)",
  gateIntro: "Your phone carries four financial apps and three of them send notifications. One reports your balance every morning at seven. One congratulates you on a streak. You could not say what any of them told you yesterday. Last week the one that mattered, the alert about a direct debit that failed, arrived in the same stack as the streak and got swiped away with it.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that replaces one of those notifications with something that happens whether or not you read it.",
  evidence: "Three randomised field experiments run with banks in Peru, Bolivia and the Philippines, sending clients text messages or letters reminding them about a savings goal. Saving rose by a modest single-digit percentage and clients were somewhat more likely to reach the goal they had named. The authors report the size plainly; the industry that cites them does not. The useful detail is that messages naming the client's own goal or a specific incentive outperformed generic ones. Limits worth stating: these were bank clients who had recently opened goal-linked accounts, so people already part way into an intention rather than the general public, the horizons were short, and message effects of this kind generally attenuate as the message becomes familiar.",
  replication: "overclaimed",
  idea: "Reminders work, at a few percent, which makes them worth switching on and never worth building a plan around.",
  why: "The mechanism is limited attention and nothing else. Saving competes for a slot against everything else in a given week, and the message puts it back into the slot. That is a real effect with a low ceiling by construction, because it can only reach the cases where you would have saved and simply did not think of it. It cannot create a surplus that does not exist and it cannot restrain a decision you are in the middle of making.\n\nWhich is precisely why the result gets oversold. A reminder is cheap to send, trivial to test, and produces a positive coefficient in almost any setting. That combination is excellent for a product roadmap and poor for a plan, and it is how a few percent becomes a home screen full of streaks.\n\nPut it beside the alternative on the same afternoon. An automatic contribution passes most of the dollar through to actual saving and keeps doing it while you ignore it. A reminder buys single digits on a balance and requires you to read it. They are not competing tools, they are ranked ones: automate everything with a fixed amount and a fixed date, then use a reminder only on the residue that genuinely cannot be automated, which is mostly one-off acts with deadlines.\n\nThe one detail worth stealing is that specific messages beat generic ones. If you are going to send yourself something, name the goal in it rather than the task.",
  failureMode: "You install a savings app with streaks and badges and a daily notification, feel organised for five weeks, and stop reading it in week six. The transfer you did not set up on that first evening would still be running today. What you have instead is one more notification you are now trained to dismiss without looking, including on the days it says something that matters.",
  worked: "Because a reminder is the fallback, the instruction has to say what it is not covering:\n\n\"Everything with a fixed amount and a fixed date becomes an automatic transfer. Whatever is left gets one calendar entry a year, on a named day, with the amount already written into the title.\"\n\nAnd if you are going to send yourself a message, take the detail the trial actually supports:\n\n\"Name the goal in the reminder, not the task.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is anything daily. A message you receive every day is a message you have trained yourself to dismiss, and the effect in the trial came from occasional messages tied to a stated goal rather than from volume.",
  experiment: "Go through every financial notification and app alert you currently receive, and count how many you acted on in the last month. Turn off the ones that scored zero. Then take whichever recurring item genuinely cannot be automated, give it one calendar entry on a named day with the amount and the goal in the title, and accept that it is the weakest instrument in this track.",
  reflection: "How many of your financial notifications had you actually acted on in the last month?",
  recall: {
    q: "Reminders raise saving by a small single-digit percentage. Why does that make them worth switching on but not worth planning around?",
    a: "Because the mechanism is attention, and attention only binds in the subset of cases where you would have saved and forgot. A message cannot manufacture surplus and cannot stop a decision you are actively making, so the ceiling is low by construction.\n\nThey get oversold because they are cheap, easy to test and reliably positive, which is ideal for a product and bad for a plan. Rank them: automatic contributions pass most of the dollar through to real saving and run while you ignore them, so automate anything with a fixed amount and date, and keep reminders for the irreducible residue. If you send one, name the goal in it, since goal-specific messages beat generic ones in the trial."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is every financial notification and reminder I currently receive. Tell me which could be replaced by an arrangement that runs whether or not I read anything, and which few are genuinely irreducible."
},
{
  id: "commitment-self-imposed-deadline",
  track: "commitment", level: "annual",
  title: "A deadline you set yourself beats no deadline and loses to one somebody else sets",
  source: "Dan Ariely and Klaus Wertenbroch, Procrastination, Deadlines, and Performance: Self-Control by Precommitment (Psychological Science, 2002)",
  gateIntro: "It is the second Saturday of March. Your mortgage is still on the rate you signed three years ago and you have known since August that it is not competitive. The insurance renewed in November on autopilot while you were away. You have said out loud, to your partner, that you would sit down and deal with all of it, and the weekend you named when you said it was in July.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that fixes a date for the annual job and names who else can see whether it happened.",
  evidence: "Two small studies on students. In one, a class allowed to choose its own deadlines for three papers, with a penalty for lateness, was compared against a class given evenly spaced deadlines by the instructor and a class free to submit everything at the end. Self-chosen deadlines beat no deadlines and lost to evenly spaced imposed ones, and most students who chose their own did not space them evenly. A second study, on a paid proofreading task with the same three conditions, produced the same ordering on errors found and on lateness. Small samples, one instructor, students rather than households, and the outcomes are essay marks and proofreading accuracy rather than money. It is very widely quoted as showing that self-imposed deadlines work, which is the first half of what it says and not the interesting half.",
  replication: "overclaimed",
  idea: "Almost nothing in your financial life has a natural due date, so the job is importing a date from outside rather than generating one yourself.",
  why: "Three conditions and three results, ranked. Being bound by somebody else was best. Binding yourself was second. Nothing at all was worst. The middle result is the one that gets quoted and the ordering is the finding.\n\nWhy self-set dates get spaced badly is worth sitting with, because it is not stupidity. At the moment you set the date you are not in the state that will later make you defer, so you cannot feel how much you will want to. And a date you set is a date you can move, silently, with no one to notice, which means it is not really a constraint so much as a strongly worded preference.\n\nThe transfer to money is unusually direct, because financial admin is the purest case of a deadline-free task. Nothing happens if you never review the mortgage. No one sends a letter if you hold two super accounts for another year. The insurance renews itself, which is worse than nothing happening. Everything expensive in this category is expensive precisely because it has no due date attached, and the only tasks that reliably get done are the ones the tax system or an employer dates for you.\n\nSo import a date. An appointment with a person, a recurring event with somebody else invited, or a date pinned to something that arrives whether or not you remember it. Self-imposing is the fallback, not the technique.",
  failureMode: "The July review happens the following March, if it happens at all. That is eight extra months on a mortgage rate you already knew was uncompetitive and a full extra year of an insurance policy you already knew was mispriced. Neither of them sent you anything, because neither of them was ever due.",
  worked: "The instruction is an appointment rather than an intention:\n\n\"Put the annual review in the calendar as a recurring event on the second Saturday of July, and invite my partner to it so that someone other than me can see whether it happened.\"\n\nStronger, where the money justifies it:\n\n\"Book the actual appointment with the broker for the second week of July, every year, because a booked appointment with a person is a date I cannot quietly move.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is an agenda. The date is the difficult part and the list is not: a review with a date and no agenda still happens, and an agenda with no date never does.",
  experiment: "Pick the financial job you do once a year, or should. Put it in the calendar on a named day with a second person invited, or book the real appointment today. If your own calendar is the only enforcement available, at least pin the day to something external that arrives anyway, such as the week after 1 July, so that the date turns up without you.",
  reflection: "Which annual job has slipped, by how many months, and whose diary is it in now?",
  recall: {
    q: "Self-imposed deadlines improved performance, so why is that the smaller half of the finding?",
    a: "Because there were three conditions and self-imposed came second. Choosing your own deadlines beat having none and lost to evenly spaced deadlines set by the instructor, and most of the people who chose their own spaced them badly. At the moment you set a date you cannot feel how much you will later want to move it, and a date you set is one you can move unobserved.\n\nFor money that matters more than usual, because financial admin has no natural due dates at all. Nothing happens if you never review anything, and the insurance renews itself. So the technique is to import a date from a person or from an external event, and self-imposing is the fallback. The studies were small, on students, and measured essays and proofreading rather than money."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are the financial jobs I should do annually and how long each has actually gone. Give me a dated July agenda, and tell me which of them will only happen if another person is in the room."
},
{
  id: "commitment-preservation-age",
  track: "commitment", level: "standing",
  title: "Australia already sold you the strictest commitment account you will ever hold, and it does not open until sixty",
  source: "Superannuation Industry (Supervision) Regulations 1994, the preservation and conditions of release rules, and the preservation age schedule reaching 60 for anyone born on or after 1 July 1964",
  gateIntro: "A colleague at lunch says he has stopped salary sacrificing because the rules keep changing and he wants his money where he can see it. You have been sacrificing two hundred a fortnight for three years and had never once framed it as a risk. You are thirty-nine. Outside super you hold about two thousand dollars, and you have never worked out how many weeks of your life that is.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the two instructions: the amount that goes in, and the condition that has to be true before you are allowed to raise it.",
  evidence: "Statute rather than a finding. Superannuation benefits are preserved, meaning they cannot be accessed until a condition of release is met, and the schedule of preservation ages rose in steps to 60 for anyone born on or after 1 July 1964, which from 1 July 2024 covers every remaining cohort. Preservation is not absolute: conditions of release include retirement after preservation age, turning 65, transition to retirement, permanent incapacity, terminal illness, severe financial hardship and compassionate grounds, and the tax treatment differs between them. It is legislation, so the age, the conditions, the caps and the tax treatment have all moved before and can move again. That is the standing risk in any commitment device enforced by somebody else's rules rather than by your own.",
  replication: "statute",
  asAt: "2026-09-02",
  sourceUrl: "https://www.ato.gov.au/tax-rates-and-codes/key-superannuation-rates-and-thresholds",
  verifiedOn: "2026-09-02",
  cheat: "Count super as the locked account you already own: preservation age is 60 for anyone born from 1 July 1964, and getting at it needs a condition of release.",
  idea: "You already hold the strongest commitment account available to you, so the live question is not whether to lock money away but how much you can afford to lock until sixty.",
  why: "Read superannuation as a commitment device rather than as a tax structure and every property the trials manipulate is present, turned up higher than any bank would dare. The restriction is enforced by law rather than by a product's goodwill. There is no early withdrawal button at all, so there is not even a penalty to weigh. And the release condition is an age, which is the one kind of deadline you cannot negotiate, move or forget.\n\nThen there is the property no trial product had. The commitment accounts in the literature were dominated: same return, less access, so taking one was a pure sacrifice of liquidity in exchange for a wall. Concessional treatment means super pays you to accept the wall. That combination is rare enough that it inverts the usual analysis, because you are normally choosing whether a constraint is worth its price and here the price is negative.\n\nWhich leaves exactly two real costs, and both are easy to skate past when the tax arithmetic looks good. The money is genuinely unreachable in an emergency that does not meet a condition of release, and the rules are statute, so the terms of the lock can change between now and the day it opens. Neither is a reason to hold nothing there. Both are reasons the emergency buffer lives somewhere else, and reasons to notice that this is the one arrangement in this track you cannot cheaply undo.\n\nThat irreversibility is why the sizing decision here deserves more care than the size of a savings transfer. A transfer you set too high gets adjusted next month. A sacrifice you set too high gets adjusted next month too, but the money already inside it stays inside it for twenty years.",
  failureMode: "Two years of aggressive sacrificing, and then eleven thousand dollars needed in a hurry for something that is not on the list of release conditions. You borrow it at a personal loan rate while your own money sits behind a wall you paid to build. Nothing malfunctioned. The lock did precisely what it was designed to do, at the one moment you did not want it to.",
  worked: "Two instructions, and the second is what keeps the first survivable:\n\n\"Set a salary sacrifice of $200 per pay into super, effective next cycle.\"\n\n\"Do not increase it until the outside account holds three months of expenses, and never fund an increase out of that account.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is a percentage of income. Elsewhere in this corpus a percentage is the better shape, because it escalates with every pay rise and needs no decision. Here the escalation runs into a one-way door, so a flat amount you raise on purpose is safer than a rate that tightens the lock while you are not looking. The day a pay rise lands is the right day to raise it, and that is a separate instruction.",
  experiment: "Work out your unavoidable monthly costs and multiply by three. Compare that against what you hold outside super in an account you could reach this week. If the outside number is smaller, the correct next instruction is a savings transfer and not a sacrifice, however good the tax arithmetic looks. If it is larger, set the sacrifice today at an amount you would not notice.",
  reflection: "How much can you reach this week outside super, and how many months of your costs is that?",
  recall: {
    q: "Super has every property a commitment savings device is supposed to have. Which property does it have that the trial products did not, and what does the lock cost you?",
    a: "It pays you to accept the restriction. The commitment accounts in the trials were dominated products, offering the same return with less access, so choosing one was a pure sacrifice of liquidity. Concessional treatment means super compensates you for the illiquidity, which makes it the rare wall that comes with a payment attached rather than a price.\n\nWhat it costs is two things worth stating plainly. The money is genuinely unreachable in an emergency that does not meet a condition of release, so borrowing at a personal loan rate while your own balance sits behind the wall is a real outcome rather than a hypothetical. And the terms are legislation: the age, the conditions, the caps and the tax treatment can all move between now and the day it opens."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is my income, my age and what I hold outside super. Tell me how much I can lock away until sixty without creating a situation where I have to borrow, and what would have to change for me to regret the decision."
},
{
  id: "commitment-one-way-account",
  track: "commitment", level: "standing",
  title: "A restriction you could undo in ten minutes still works, because the impulse does not last ten minutes",
  source: "David Laibson, Golden Eggs and Hyperbolic Discounting (Quarterly Journal of Economics, 1997) for the argument, and ordinary retail banking arrangements for the build",
  gateIntro: "You moved the savings to a second bank in April and it worked exactly as intended. Then in June a plumber wanted paying that afternoon, so you added the account as a saved payee to get the money across quickly. It is now September, the payee is still sitting there, and the balance is four hundred dollars below what the transfers say it should be, across three withdrawals you cannot immediately place.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction as a list of what must not exist: which links, cards and saved payees are deleted, and what replaces them in an emergency.",
  evidence: "No trial. Laibson's argument is theoretical: where preferences reverse as the near date arrives, an asset that cannot be consumed on impulse is genuinely valuable, and a household can rationally hold one even at a cost in return. No experiment tests the specific assembly described here, which is what an Australian has to build in the absence of a product shaped like it: a second institution, no card, no saved payee, no linked transfer. The nearest real evidence is the commitment savings trials, and every one of those tested a bank-enforced restriction rather than a self-imposed one you could dismantle in ten minutes. Treat this as engineering informed by a theory and by adjacent trials, and not as a tested arrangement.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "The barrier does not need to be permanent, it needs to outlast the window in which the decision to raid the account gets made.",
  why: "The standard objection to self-imposed friction is that it does not bind, because the person who built the wall holds the ladder. That is true and it is beside the point. Look at when savings accounts actually get emptied. At a checkout. In a browser tab at eleven at night. Mid-conversation, while someone waits for an answer. Those windows are seconds to minutes long, and a barrier that takes longer than the window survives it without ever having to be permanent.\n\nWhich makes the design problem completeness rather than height. People remove one route and consider the job done. The routes are the linked transfer inside a single app, the saved payee, the debit card attached to the savings account, the card sitting in a phone wallet, an overdraft, and an offset arrangement that puts the balance next to the spending account on the same screen. Each is independent, and closing five of six leaves the account fully reachable in eleven seconds.\n\nThe cost of doing this properly is a day of delay in a genuine emergency, and that cost is the reason the emergency buffer does not live behind the wall. Putting the emergency fund in the least reachable place is the most common way this idea gets implemented backwards. The reachable buffer comes first, at something like three months of costs, and only the surplus above it goes somewhere slow.\n\nLaibson's contribution to this is the claim that the illiquidity is worth paying for, which has a corollary people skip: chasing a quarter of a percent of interest into an account you can empty from your everyday app is optimising the smaller number. The rate difference is basis points. The account you can raid instantly is a different order of loss.",
  failureMode: "You move the money to another bank, feel organised, and three weeks later add a saved payee because one transfer was awkward once. The wall now has a door in it that opens in eleven seconds, and you will not notice the difference until you are looking at a balance four hundred dollars lower than your own transfers imply, unable to reconstruct where it went.",
  worked: "This instruction is a list of absences, because removing one route leaves the others standing:\n\n\"The savings sit at an institution where I hold nothing else. No debit card issued. Not saved as a payee anywhere. Not linked inside my everyday banking app. Money in is automatic. Money out is typed from scratch.\"\n\nThen the clause that stops the whole thing being a bad idea:\n\n\"The emergency buffer does not live here. It stays in the reachable account at three months of costs, so this account is never the answer to something urgent.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any penalty or fixed term. Everything on that list costs time and nothing else, which is what makes it survivable, and none of it has a trial behind it.",
  experiment: "Time yourself. From your phone's lock screen, move one dollar out of your savings account and count the seconds. Then close one route: delete the saved payee, cancel the card, or unlink the account. Time it again. If the second number is not at least a few minutes, you closed a route you were not using, so find the one you actually used and close that instead.",
  reflection: "How many seconds did it take before, and which route turned out to be the fast one?",
  forecast: { q: "Will every fast path I deleted today still be deleted in ninety days?" },
  recall: {
    q: "A self-imposed restriction can be undone by the person who imposed it, so why is it worth building at all?",
    a: "Because the decisions that drain a savings account are made inside short windows: at a checkout, in a browser tab late at night, mid-conversation while somebody waits. A barrier only has to outlast the window, and ten minutes of friction against a thirty-second impulse is enough. Permanence is not the requirement.\n\nWhat matters instead is completeness. The linked transfer, the saved payee, the debit card, the wallet entry and the overdraft are independent routes, and closing most of them still leaves the account reachable in seconds. There is no trial behind this specific assembly, since the commitment savings trials all tested a bank-enforced restriction rather than a self-imposed one, and the buffer for genuine emergencies has to stay outside the wall."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is where my money sits and every way I can move it. Enumerate the fast paths out of my savings, rank them by how often I have actually used each one, and tell me which to close first without leaving me stuck in a real emergency."
},
{
  id: "commitment-remove-the-stored-card",
  track: "commitment", level: "standing",
  title: "Deleting the stored card is a larger intervention than deciding to spend less",
  source: "Removal of stored payment credentials from browsers, phones and merchant accounts, against the commercial history of one-click purchasing and card-on-file retention",
  gateIntro: "The statement carries eleven card transactions under sixty dollars each and you can account for four of them. One is a phone case. One is a subscription you were certain you had cancelled. It is Sunday night and you are trying to reconstruct a month from a list, and the detail you keep returning to is that not one of those eleven purchases involved typing a card number.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction as a deletion: which stored cards go, from where, and what you say when a checkout offers to save one.",
  evidence: "No trial that this entry rests on. There is no experiment I can point to that isolates removing a saved card from a merchant account and measures what happens to spending. What exists is the revealed behaviour of the sellers: one-click purchasing was patented and fought over, card-on-file is tracked as a retention metric, and wallets are pushed hardest at the exact moment of checkout. That is strong evidence that the friction matters to people with large samples and a revenue incentive, which is not the same as a measured effect on you. The defaults literature is consistent with it, being the same finding read backwards, since if removing steps raises take-up then adding them should lower it. Consistent is not tested.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Every stored card is a default you did not choose, and it is the only category of default in your life that is deliberately optimised against you by professionals.",
  why: "Count the design effort on each side of a checkout. On the seller's side: card on file, one-click, wallet, saved address, pre-ticked subscription renewal, a confirmation screen tested against millions of sessions. On yours: an intention to be careful. If defaults are the strongest mechanism in personal finance, then defaults chosen by a counterparty with an analytics team are the strongest mechanism running against you, and they are running continuously.\n\nThis is also the highest-yield friction available because of where it sits. Saving can be automated, so the saving side of the problem has a machine. Not buying cannot be automated: there is no instruction that executes a non-purchase. The only lever you have on purchases you have not thought of yet is the number of steps between wanting one and having it, which means the spending side of this site is almost entirely about step counts.\n\nWhat deleting the card actually does is convert an impulse into a task. Typing sixteen digits is trivial, and the triviality is the point: it is trivial and it takes ninety seconds, and an impulse does not reliably survive ninety seconds of looking for your wallet. It also drags the amount back into view, which a stored card is specifically designed to avoid.\n\nThe honest limit is that this mechanism has nothing to say about large planned purchases. Nobody buys a car because the card was saved. It works on the eleven small ones you cannot remember a fortnight later, which for most people is where the leak actually is, and there is no trial behind it.",
  failureMode: "Eleven purchases in a month, none over sixty dollars, none of them recalled two weeks later, together larger than the automatic transfer you had been congratulating yourself about. Each of them took under fifteen seconds, because the card was stored, the address was stored, and nothing between the thought and the confirmation asked you a single question.",
  worked: "The instruction is a deletion list, and it has to name the places people forget:\n\n\"Remove the saved card from every merchant account, from the browser's autofill, and from the phone's wallet for everything except transport and groceries.\"\n\nThen the clause that stops it quietly reversing itself:\n\n\"When a checkout offers to save the card, the answer is no, every time, and I type it again next time.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is a budget or a category limit. Those need you to be paying attention at the moment of spending, and that moment has been designed by other people specifically to get past your attention. A deleted card works while you are not paying attention, which is the only sort of arrangement this site trusts.",
  experiment: "Open the payment settings of the three retailers you use most, plus your browser's autofill, and delete every stored card. It takes about ten minutes. Write down the date, because the measurement that matters is how long it takes before one of them is stored again, and which merchant did it without asking you.",
  reflection: "How many places was your card stored, and how many of those had you deliberately chosen?",
  recall: {
    q: "Re-entering a card takes under two minutes, so why is deleting the stored one a stronger tool than deciding to spend less?",
    a: "Because a decision to spend less has to be recalled at the moment of spending, and that moment is engineered by people whose job is to compress it. A stored card is a default, and on the spending side of your life the defaults were chosen by counterparties to raise conversion. Deleting one converts an impulse into a task, and the task outlasts the impulse.\n\nIt is also the only lever on that side of the ledger, because saving can be automated and not buying cannot. What is missing is evidence: no trial isolates card removal and measures spending. The support is the sellers' own behaviour, given how hard one-click and card-on-file are optimised, plus the defaults literature read in reverse, and it says nothing about large planned purchases."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are the retailers and services I buy from most often. Tell me where my card is likely stored, what order to remove them in, and which of those removals will annoy me enough that I quietly undo it."
},
{
  id: "commitment-waiting-rule",
  track: "commitment", level: "standing",
  title: "A written waiting rule above a threshold makes you decide the same purchase twice",
  source: "Self-imposed cooling-off rules on discretionary purchases, and the statutory cooling-off periods that exist in Australian consumer and property law for the same reason",
  gateIntro: "It is nine on a Thursday night at the end of a week that went badly. Two tabs are open on a four hundred dollar item you first looked at three weeks ago and had talked yourself out of twice. Delivery is free if you order within the next two hours, which is a coincidence that somebody arranged. Your thumb is over the button and you have already told yourself you have earned it.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the rule as a sentence with a dollar threshold and a number of nights in it, and say what happens when the wait ends.",
  evidence: "No trial. Nothing I can point to isolates a self-imposed waiting rule on personal purchases and measures the result. Two things sit adjacent to it and neither is the same claim. Statutory cooling-off periods exist in Australian consumer and property law, which tells you legislators believe a delay changes decisions where regret is expensive, and a legislative belief is not an experiment on you. And the deadline evidence shows self-imposed constraints do something real while also showing they are the weaker kind, which is exactly the weakness of a rule whose only enforcement is that you wrote it down. Craft, with a plausible mechanism and no measurement.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "A delay does not stop a purchase you actually want, it stops the ones you would not have made twice.",
  why: "The rule works by separating the decision from the state that produced it. Nearly every regretted purchase above a threshold was made in a particular condition: the end of a hard week, the middle of a comparison, an hour after a good result, three drinks in. None of those conditions lasts as long as the item does. A waiting rule is not a ban and does not evaluate anything. It requires the purchase to be approved by two different states of you, on two different days.\n\nThe threshold matters more than the duration, and this is where people set it wrong. A rule that applies to everything gets broken on a twelve dollar purchase within a week, and once broken it is broken, because the only thing holding it up was that you had never violated it. Set the number high enough that the rule applies a handful of times a year, so each application is an actual event.\n\nThe cost is unusually easy to reason about. For the purchases you go on to make anyway, the rule costs you a delay and nothing else. For the ones you do not make, it costs nothing at all. There is no version of this that loses you money, which is rare in this field and is the main argument for it, since the evidence is absent.\n\nThe weakness is honest and worth stating: the enforcement is a note you wrote. The deadline literature is fairly clear that self-imposed constraints are weaker than imposed ones, so bolt on whatever external component is available. Telling one other person what the rule says is free and is the only external enforcement most purchases will ever get.",
  failureMode: "A four hundred dollar purchase made at nine on a Thursday night, delivered on Tuesday, still in its box in October. Three of those a year is more than a thousand dollars, and you have spent whole weekends comparing savings accounts over considerably less than that.",
  worked: "The rule needs a number and a duration, and the number should be higher than feels natural:\n\n\"Anything discretionary over $300 waits three nights. I write the item and the date in the same note every time, and if I still want it on the fourth day I buy it with no further argument.\"\n\nThat last clause is what keeps the rule alive. A waiting period that turns into a fresh deliberation every time is exhausting and gets dropped, so the deal has to be that waiting ends in permission.\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any judgement about whether the purchase is sensible. The rule evaluates nothing. It only insists that two different versions of you agree, because agreement between states is the only thing a note can actually enforce.",
  experiment: "Set the threshold today at a number that would have caught between three and six purchases in the last year rather than sixty. Write the rule, with its duration, into the notes app you actually open, and tell one other person what it says. Then go back through twelve months of statements and count what it would have caught, so you know whether the threshold is anywhere useful.",
  reflection: "What threshold did you set, and how many purchases from the last year would it have caught?",
  forecast: { q: "Will my next discretionary purchase over the threshold actually wait the full period?" },
  recall: {
    q: "A waiting rule has no enforcement beyond a note you wrote. What is it doing that a decision to be careful is not?",
    a: "It requires the purchase to be approved by two different states of you on two different days. Regretted purchases above a threshold are usually made in a specific condition that expires faster than the item does, and the delay does not evaluate the purchase, it simply refuses to let one condition decide alone.\n\nThe threshold matters more than the duration, since a rule that covers everything gets broken on something trivial and is then broken for good. There is no trial behind any of this. The adjacent facts are that statutory cooling-off periods exist where regret is expensive, and that self-imposed constraints measure as real but weaker than imposed ones, which is precisely this rule's weakness."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is a year of my discretionary spending. Tell me what threshold a waiting rule should sit at to catch the purchases I regretted without catching the ones I did not, and how many it would have caught."
},
{
  id: "commitment-card-versus-cash",
  track: "commitment", level: "judgement",
  title: "You probably pay more with a card than with cash, and the size of that gap has not held up",
  source: "Drazen Prelec and Duncan Simester, Always Leave Home Without It: A Further Investigation of the Credit-Card Effect on Willingness to Pay (Marketing Letters, 2001)",
  gateIntro: "Somebody at work is halfway through explaining that studies show you spend twice as much with a card, and is recommending you withdraw a week of cash every Monday and live off it. You have heard the claim before. You have also just paid for parking at a machine with no coin slot, and the last time you tried carrying cash for a week you gave up on the Wednesday.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction that caps discretionary spending using a balance rather than a feeling, with the amount and the day in it.",
  evidence: "Prelec and Simester ran sealed-bid auctions for real tickets to professional sporting events, with bidders randomly assigned to pay by cash or by credit card, and the card bidders bid substantially more. The much-quoted claim that they bid roughly twice as much rests on a small auction with few participants. The replication history is mixed: later work on the pain of paying and on payment-method effects reports smaller effects, sometimes none, often concentrated in particular categories, and the headline magnitude has not reliably reproduced. The stimulus has also moved underneath the finding, because the 2001 comparison was cash against physically handing over a credit card, and the comparison a reader faces now is a phone against a phone. The direction is plausible. The size is not something to plan around.",
  replication: "contested",
  idea: "Use the payment method to set a limit rather than to create pain, because a balance that runs out is arithmetic and the pain of paying is contested.",
  why: "The original mechanism is coupling, and it is a good story. Cash ties the payment to the purchase in time, makes the outflow visible, and leaves you with a countable amount less than you had. A card decouples the two, defers the pain to a statement, and shows you nothing. That predicts the direction of the effect and it is why the finding was believed so readily.\n\nWhat did not survive is the magnitude. Small samples, one unusual stimulus, and a subsequent literature that finds the effect smaller, patchier and sometimes absent. This is a textbook case of a headline number outliving its evidence, and it still gets quoted in personal finance writing as though it were a constant. Worse, the thing that was measured barely exists now: nobody in the original design was comparing a phone against a phone.\n\nSo the useful move is to stop relying on the sensation and build the constraint out of a balance. A separate everyday account with its own card and a fixed weekly transfer into it produces a hard limit that does not require you to feel anything on the day. When it is empty the card declines, and a declined card is a fact rather than a feeling. That converts a contested psychological effect into arithmetic you cannot argue with.\n\nAnd this is one of the places where knowing the evidence is weak is worth as much as the finding. A cash-only rule has a real compliance cost, gives up fraud protection and rewards, and fails at the first parking machine. Building it on a number that has not held up is how a plausible idea becomes nine days of inconvenience and nothing else.",
  failureMode: "You go cash-only for discretionary spending, hold the line for nine days, and abandon it at the first machine that takes cards only. What you actually needed was a second account with two hundred dollars landing in it every Monday, which requires no discipline whatsoever and would still be running in two years.",
  worked: "The instruction turns the psychology into arithmetic:\n\n\"Open a separate everyday account with its own card, transfer $200 into it every Monday, and use only that card for discretionary spending.\"\n\nThe constraint is now the balance instead of the sensation. When the account is empty the card declines, which needs no willpower and happens whether or not you are paying attention that day.\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any instruction to use cash. The evidence that cash makes you spend less is contested, the compliance cost is high and rising, and a weekly top-up costs nothing to maintain. If you want the pain of paying as well, you can have it for free by leaving the main account's card off your phone.",
  experiment: "Open a second everyday account today if you do not have one, and set a weekly transfer into it for discretionary spending. Take the amount from your last three months of statements rather than from an intention, because an aspirational number is how this arrangement dies in week three. Then put that card at the front of your phone's wallet and take the main account's card off the phone entirely.",
  reflection: "What weekly amount did you pick, and how does it compare with what you actually spent over the last three months?",
  recall: {
    q: "The claim that people pay far more by card than by cash is quoted everywhere. What is wrong with it, and what should you build instead?",
    a: "The direction is plausible and the magnitude is not settled. It comes from small sealed-bid auctions for sporting tickets where bidders assigned to pay by card bid substantially more, and the repeated near-doubling rests on very few participants. Later payment-method work finds smaller, patchier and sometimes absent effects, and the stimulus itself has changed, because the original comparison was cash against handing over a card and the modern one is a phone against a phone.\n\nSo do not build a cash-only rule, which fails on convenience within weeks and gives up fraud protection and rewards. Build the limit out of a balance: a separate everyday account, its own card, a fixed weekly transfer. When it empties, the card declines, which turns a contested psychological effect into arithmetic that does not need you to feel anything."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are three months of my discretionary spending. Set the weekly top-up for a separate spending account at a number that is realistic rather than aspirational, and tell me what I will actually do in the weeks it runs out early."
},
{
  id: "commitment-sludge-audit",
  track: "commitment", level: "annual",
  title: "Every arrangement you cannot exit in five minutes was built that way on purpose, and finding them is worth an afternoon a year",
  source: "Cass Sunstein's work on sludge and sludge audits, Richard Thaler's coining of the term, and the design of retention flows in subscription and insurance products",
  gateIntro: "The insurance renewal email arrived three weeks ago with the new premium in the third paragraph. You noticed it was higher, meant to look into it, and it renewed on Tuesday while you were in a meeting. The comparison would take twenty minutes. Cancelling, as you already know from last time, requires a phone number answered between nine and five, and you are on calls between nine and five.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the instruction for the annual audit: the date it happens, the test you apply to each arrangement, and what you do with anything that fails it.",
  evidence: "No trial. The term sludge is Thaler's, for friction that works against the person facing it, and Sunstein's work proposes auditing institutions for it. The real evidence underneath is the defaults literature read in reverse: if removing steps from a process raises completion, adding steps lowers it, and there is a substantial body of work showing take-up of programmes falling as paperwork burden rises. What has no trial behind it is the self-administered version proposed here, in which you audit your own arrangements rather than an institution's. Nobody has measured whether a household doing this changes anything. The argument for it is structural rather than empirical: the friction in those arrangements was designed by the counterparty, so it points away from you by construction.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Friction is an instrument, and the arrangements you already hold are full of it pointing the wrong way, because somebody else installed it and they were not thinking about you.",
  why: "The symmetry is the whole entry. Everything else in this track installs friction in front of your own worse decisions. The same instrument has already been installed in front of your better ones by every party that profits from your inertia. Automatic renewal. A retention queue. A cancellation path that requires a call in business hours. A fee-free account that stays fee-free only if you log in each quarter. A fund whose rollover needs a form and a certified copy of something. None of that is incompetence. It is this site's central finding, used by people who read the same papers, in the other direction.\n\nIt has to be an afternoon rather than a standing instruction, which is unusual here and worth being explicit about. Sludge is deliberately non-standard: if it were uniform, somebody would have automated exiting it. Each item has to be found and pushed through by hand, which is why this is the one entry in the track that genuinely wants a diarised date and a couple of hours rather than a rule that runs without you.\n\nThe test that makes it operational is minutes to exit. Go through every recurring arrangement and write down how long leaving it would take. Anything over five minutes is either a deliberate barrier or an accident that behaves exactly like one, and the distinction does not matter because the effect on you is identical.\n\nWhat the audit produces is one of two things, and the second is undervalued. Either you exit while you have the list in front of you and the momentum to sit on hold, or you write down that the exit is expensive, which is information about how carefully the next arrangement of that kind should be chosen at the moment of signing.",
  failureMode: "The insurance renews for a fourth year at a premium well above what the same insurer quotes a new customer, because cancelling needs a phone call during the hours you are working. None of that is accidental, it is a designed retention flow, and those four years cost you more than every savings account decision you made in the same period put together.",
  worked: "The instruction is a dated audit with one fixed test:\n\n\"On the second Saturday of July, list every recurring payment and every account, and beside each write how many minutes exiting it would take. Anything over five minutes is either exited that afternoon or gets a written reason why not.\"\n\nAnd the clause that makes next year's audit cheaper:\n\n\"Before signing anything new, ask how it is cancelled, and write the answer down at the time.\"\n\nWhat is deliberately absent is any attempt to automate the audit itself. The friction is non-standard by design, which is exactly why it cannot be handed to a standing instruction, and this is the only entry here that asks you for an afternoon instead of ten minutes.",
  experiment: "Pull three months of statements and list every recurring payment. Beside each, write the number of minutes cancelling would take, and mark the ones that need a phone call during business hours. Then cancel one today, the worst ratio of cost to usefulness on the list, while you still have the list open and the momentum to wait on hold.",
  reflection: "How many of your recurring arrangements take more than five minutes to exit, and how many need a phone call?",
  recall: {
    q: "You spend this whole track installing friction against yourself. What is the annual audit looking for, and why can it not be automated?",
    a: "The same instrument installed by somebody else, pointing the other way. Automatic renewal, retention queues, cancellation paths that require a business-hours call, accounts with conditions you must maintain: none of it is incompetence, it is the defaults finding aimed at your inertia by people who profit from it.\n\nIt cannot be automated because sludge is deliberately non-standard, and anything uniform would already have a tool for exiting it. So it wants a diarised afternoon and a single test, minutes to exit, with anything over five minutes either exited on the spot or recorded with a reason. No trial has measured a household doing this. The support is the defaults literature in reverse, plus the work on take-up falling as paperwork rises."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is every recurring payment and account I hold. Estimate how long exiting each one takes, flag the ones designed to be hard to leave, and tell me which two to cancel this afternoon."
},
{
  id: "commitment-tightest-you-will-not-defect-from",
  track: "commitment", level: "judgement",
  title: "The right restriction is the tightest one you will not cancel, which is rarely the tightest one available",
  source: "The gap between take-up rates, forfeiture rates and abandonment in the commitment device literature, read as a design constraint",
  gateIntro: "In January you put three years of savings into a five-year term deposit and felt decisive about it. In month seven your father needed a flight to Perth at short notice and a bathroom decided to leak in the same fortnight. You broke the term, lost the interest, and have since told two separate people that locking money away does not work for you. The money is back in the everyday account as of Friday.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the restriction you would install now, one step looser than the one that failed, and the event that would make you cancel it.",
  evidence: "No trial settles this, and saying so is the entry. Two findings pull in opposite directions with nothing measured between them. In an allocation experiment, people put more money into accounts carrying harsher early withdrawal penalties, which argues for tightening. In the field, take-up of commitment products is a minority and forfeiture among the takers of a deposit contract was substantial, which says tightness carries a real cost paid by the people who accept it. What nobody has measured is the shape of your own curve: how tight a restriction can be before you abandon the arrangement rather than merely strain against it. This is a design rule inferred from the boundary conditions of other people's trials, and it should be held exactly that loosely.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "An arrangement that is too tight does not get broken, it gets abandoned, and an abandoned arrangement returns nothing at all for the rest of the decade.",
  why: "The two failure modes are not symmetric, and that asymmetry is the whole argument. A restriction slightly looser than optimal leaks some money. A restriction tighter than you can live with gets switched off completely, and then leaks all of it plus every contribution you would have made afterwards, plus the belief that arrangements of this kind work for you. When the downside is that lopsided, the optimum sits well below the maximum, and further below than it feels like it should.\n\nOver-restriction fails in three recognisable ways. The amount is sized to a good month and dishonours in a bad one. The lock catches money you genuinely needed, and you learn that the arrangement is dangerous rather than that the term was too long. Or the structure becomes elaborate enough that maintaining it is a chore, and chores end quietly without anyone deciding anything.\n\nSo the method is a ratchet rather than a setting. Start at a restriction you are confident of, run it through at least one bad month, and only then tighten by a step small enough that failing costs you the step rather than the arrangement. That is the opposite of how these get built, which is at maximum, on day one, in January, in a burst of enthusiasm that is itself a temporary state making decisions for a permanent one.\n\nAnd this is why no instruction can carry the entry. The tightest restriction you will not defect from is a fact about you that you do not currently have and cannot look up. The only way to learn it is to run something and watch. The most useful thing available in advance is to write down what would make you cancel, because that sentence is the design specification and almost nobody writes it.",
  failureMode: "Three years of savings locked into a five-year term in January, needed in month seven, broken with the interest forfeited. The conclusion you drew was that locking money away is not for you, and the conclusion actually available was that five years was two years too many. The cost of the wrong lesson is every arrangement you now will not try.",
  worked: "The instruction is a ratchet with a written stopping condition:\n\n\"Start at one day of delay and no card attached. If that holds for six months including at least one bad month, add a notice period. If I ever cancel a restriction, the next version I install is the one below it, not the one above.\"\n\nThen the clause almost nobody writes, which is worth more than the rest of it:\n\n\"The thing that would make me cancel this is _____.\"\n\nFilled in honestly, that sentence is the specification. What is deliberately absent is any target level of restriction, because the right level is a fact about you that you do not have yet, and the only instrument that measures it is a bad month.",
  experiment: "Write down the restriction you currently have and the one you were about to install, then answer one question in writing: what specific event would make you cancel it? If you cannot name an event, the arrangement is untested rather than safe. Install the version one step below the one you were about to install, and diarise a date six months out to consider tightening it.",
  reflection: "What event would make you cancel your current arrangement, and had you ever written it down before today?",
  forecast: { q: "Will the restriction I install today still be in place, unchanged or tighter, in six months?" },
  recall: {
    q: "Why does the best restriction sit below the tightest one you could impose rather than at it?",
    a: "Because the failure modes are asymmetric. Slightly too loose leaks a little money. Too tight gets abandoned, and an abandoned arrangement loses everything in it plus every contribution you would have made afterwards, plus your willingness to try the next one. A lopsided downside pulls the optimum well below the maximum.\n\nSo the method is a ratchet: start where you are confident, survive one bad month, then tighten by a step small enough that failure costs a step rather than the whole arrangement. No trial settles where your line is. The allocation experiment says people want taller walls, the field take-up and forfeiture rates say tightness has a price, and nobody has measured the point at which you personally walk away."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is the commitment arrangement I am about to set up. Tell me which clause is most likely to make me cancel the whole thing, what the version one step looser looks like, and what event I should write down as my cancel trigger."
}
);
